Run one explicitly selected program inside the native Cageforge sandbox.

The TOML profile supplies the access policy: system paths to read, application paths to write, environment rules, network destinations, and timeout. The command after `--` is passed as argv. One invocation creates one boundary around the program and all of its descendants.

The native backend is selected automatically from the target OS. On Linux, `linux-bundled-bubblewrap` also embeds the verified Bubblewrap resource. There is no unsandboxed fallback.

Preflight approval is disabled unless the selected TOML profile sets `approval.mode = "preflight"`. In preflight mode the CLI shows the exact command capabilities before launch. `--approve` is for a trusted host and never bypasses the policy ceiling; non-interactive runs without an approval fail closed. Persistent approvals are stored only when the profile selects persistent persistence.

The `permissions` subcommands list one bounded page or revoke host-owned persistent approvals without changing a running sandbox.

Usage: cageforge-cli <COMMAND>

Commands:
  run          Resolve a TOML profile and run one explicit program
  schema       Print the JSON schema for Cageforge TOML profiles
  permissions  Inspect and revoke host-owned persistent permission grants
  help         Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

EXAMPLES:
  cageforge-cli run --config sandbox.toml --profile isolated -- untrusted-program --safe-mode
  cageforge-cli run --config sandbox.toml --profile build -- cargo test --workspace
  cageforge-cli run --config permission-preflight.toml --approve --permission-store /path/to/permissions.json -- tool
  cageforge-cli permissions list --page-size 50
  cageforge-cli permissions revoke --id <grant-id>
  cageforge-cli permissions revoke-all --yes
  cageforge-cli schema

`--permission-store PATH` explicitly selects the host-owned persistent grant store and takes priority. Without it, the CLI uses the current user's native Cageforge state directory. The file is created only after a persistent approval; deleting it revokes saved approvals. Listing cursors are valid only while the store document remains unchanged.

The CLI is a thin adapter. For native host requirements and the library API, see:
  https://github.com/m62624/cageforge/tree/main/crates/cageforge-linux
  https://github.com/m62624/cageforge/tree/main/crates/cageforge-windows
  https://github.com/m62624/cageforge/tree/main/crates/cageforge-macos
